A comparison needs the same gas state at both ports (@gas-gauge-comparison: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @industrial-gases-and-vacuum, #source "../sources/Ionization-Gauge-Calibration-Fedchak.lingua#r_18KG5KWT64MPYQ4D47V1T99CKW", #source "../sources/Total-and-Partial-Pressure-Jousten.lingua#r_7SH9NGBWVVK5YN24S2VZPTW45M", #industrial-gases-and-vacuum "../../../inquiry/metrology/calibration/Response-and-regression.lingua#r_182XY9TP839DMYS0DM3ZEGQ3BH") { r_5T0JCXM3DS5RCAXM9YRTX261ZY
**Read:** [Calibration of ionization vacuum gauges — Fedchak and colleagues](../sources/Ionization-Gauge-Calibration-Fedchak.lingua#r_18KG5KWT64MPYQ4D47V1T99CKW), complete apparatus and comparison sections; [Total and Partial Pressure Measurement — Karl Jousten](../sources/Total-and-Partial-Pressure-Jousten.lingua#r_7SH9NGBWVVK5YN24S2VZPTW45M), §3.2.

Two instruments in one chamber do not automatically sample the same gas state. Port geometry, inlet/outlet placement, temperature, local sources and pumping can create gradients or directional molecular populations. The comparison must establish sufficiently equivalent conditions at the actual measurement interfaces.

Distinguish static and flowing comparisons. A closed chamber can drift through wall release or gauge pumping; a flowing chamber can have spatial gradients. Stability at one reference instrument does not prove uniformity everywhere. Swapping locations or adding observations can reveal a systematic difference that repeated readings at one configuration miss.

Record the gauge head, controller, cables, mounting and programmed parameters as the measured system. A calibration of one configuration cannot silently be assigned to a changed one. The historical standards cited by a paper explain its procedure; a present standards claim requires the actual applicable edition.

**Work:** Draw a comparison arrangement and identify three ways it could produce agreement for the wrong reason. Propose a change of geometry, gas or operating condition that tests the most consequential assumption. Separate a reference's calibration uncertainty from the transfer arrangement's additional uncertainty.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
**Continue in gas and vacuum systems:** [Fit a response relation and inspect what the residuals say](../../../inquiry/metrology/calibration/Response-and-regression.lingua#r_182XY9TP839DMYS0DM3ZEGQ3BH).

